{
  "title": "Straight on, then turn right",
  "title_target": "Düz git, sonra sağa dön",
  "goal": "Give commands and prohibitions, make suggestions, and ask for and understand directions.",
  "bekal": [
    "forming *gel!*, *gelin!*, *gelme!*, *gelmeyin!* and *gidelim!*",
    "asking the way and understanding the answer",
    "using *lütfen* and the *-ir misiniz* form to soften commands"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Dialogue — Yol tarifi",
      "intro": "Budi is lost in Beyoğlu, looking for a bookshop. He stops a woman on the sidewalk.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Affedersiniz, bir şey sorabilir miyim?",
          "id": "Excuse me, may I ask something?",
          "literal": "forgive-you(formal), one thing can-ask question-I?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Kadın:",
          "text": "Tabii, buyurun.",
          "id": "Of course, go ahead.",
          "literal": "of-course, please-go-ahead."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Robinson Kitabevi nerede, biliyor musunuz?",
          "id": "Do you know where the Robinson Bookshop is?",
          "literal": "Robinson Book-house-its where, know question-you(formal)?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Kadın:",
          "text": "Biliyorum. Buradan düz gidin, yaklaşık iki yüz metre.",
          "id": "I do. Go straight on from here, about two hundred meters.",
          "literal": "know-I. here-from straight go-you(formal), about two hundred meter."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Düz. Tamam.",
          "id": "Straight on. OK.",
          "literal": "straight. OK."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Kadın:",
          "text": "Sonra ikinci sokaktan sağa dönün. Köşede bir eczane var.",
          "id": "Then turn right at the second street. There's a pharmacy on the corner.",
          "literal": "then second street-from right-to turn-you(formal). corner-at a pharmacy there-is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Eczaneden sonra?",
          "id": "After the pharmacy?",
          "literal": "pharmacy-from after?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Kadın:",
          "text": "Biraz yürüyün. Kitabevi solda, sinemanın yanında.",
          "id": "Walk a little way. The bookshop is on the left, next to the cinema.",
          "literal": "a-little walk-you(formal). book-house left-on, cinema-of side-its-at."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Yürüyerek ne kadar sürer?",
          "id": "How long does it take on foot?",
          "literal": "walking how much lasts?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Kadın:",
          "text": "Beş dakika. Kaybolmayın, tabela büyük.",
          "id": "Five minutes. You won't get lost — the sign is big.",
          "literal": "five minute. don't-get-lost-you(formal), sign big."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Çok teşekkür ederim, çok yardımcı oldunuz.",
          "id": "Thank you so much, you've been very helpful.",
          "literal": "very thanks do-I, very helpful became-you(formal)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Kadın:",
          "text": "Rica ederim. Kolay gelsin!",
          "id": "You're welcome. Kolay gelsin!",
          "literal": "request do-I. easy may-it-come!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Affedersiniz is the standard opener with strangers.** Literally \"you forgive\", it's used just like \"excuse me\" — to stop someone, to get past, or to catch a waiter's attention.",
        "**Commands in the siz form sound polite, not bossy.** *Gidin*, *dönün*, *yürüyün* are the normal way Turks give directions to strangers. The *sen* form (*git*, *dön*) is only for friends, children or younger people.",
        "**Yürüyerek means \"on foot\".** The suffix *-erek* describes how something is done; it's A2 material, but you can already use two forms as whole words: *yürüyerek* (on foot) and *koşarak* (running).",
        "**Kolay gelsin** (\"may it come easy\") is said to anyone who's working — cashiers, builders, drivers, waiters, even a stranger carrying boxes. English has no exact equivalent, and nothing sounds more Turkish."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The imperative",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Turkish imperative is the simplest form there is: for *sen*, just the stem, with nothing added. For *siz*, add *-in / -ın / -un / -ün*."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "sen",
            "siz",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "gelmek",
              "gel!",
              "gelin!",
              "come!"
            ],
            [
              "gitmek",
              "git!",
              "gidin!",
              "go!"
            ],
            [
              "bakmak",
              "bak!",
              "bakın!",
              "look!"
            ],
            [
              "oturmak",
              "otur!",
              "oturun!",
              "sit!"
            ],
            [
              "dönmek",
              "dön!",
              "dönün!",
              "turn!"
            ],
            [
              "beklemek",
              "bekle!",
              "bekleyin!",
              "wait!"
            ],
            [
              "okumak",
              "oku!",
              "okuyun!",
              "read!"
            ],
            [
              "yardım etmek",
              "yardım et!",
              "yardım edin!",
              "help!"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "There's an even more formal form, *-iniz*: *geliniz*, *buyurunuz*. It's used on signs, in airport announcements and in official letters — rarely in conversation."
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "Prohibitions"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "sen",
            "siz",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "gelme!",
              "gelmeyin!",
              "don't come!"
            ],
            [
              "gitme!",
              "gitmeyin!",
              "don't go!"
            ],
            [
              "unutma!",
              "unutmayın!",
              "don't forget!"
            ],
            [
              "korkma!",
              "korkmayın!",
              "don't be afraid!"
            ],
            [
              "merak etme!",
              "merak etmeyin!",
              "don't worry!"
            ],
            [
              "acele etme!",
              "acele etmeyin!",
              "don't rush!"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Let's — the -elim form",
          "items": [
            "To suggest doing something together, use *-elim / -alım*: *gidelim!* (let's go), *başlayalım!* (let's start), *bakalım* (let's see), *görüşelim* (let's meet).",
            "*Bakalım* is used far more widely than its meaning suggests: it closes a sentence as \"we'll see\" — *Bakalım ne olacak.*"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "Softened commands"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A bare command is rarely used with adults you don't know. Three ways to soften it, from lightest to most polite:"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "Example",
            "How it feels"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "command + lütfen",
              "Bekleyin lütfen.",
              "polite, neutral"
            ],
            [
              "-ir misiniz",
              "Bekler misiniz?",
              "polite, asking"
            ],
            [
              "-ebilir misiniz",
              "Bekleyebilir misiniz?",
              "most polite"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Asking for and giving directions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Türkçe",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Affedersiniz, ... nerede?",
              "Excuse me, where is ...?"
            ],
            [
              "... nasıl gidebilirim?",
              "How can I get to ...?"
            ],
            [
              "Buraya uzak mı?",
              "Is it far from here?"
            ],
            [
              "Yürüyerek ne kadar sürer?",
              "How long is it on foot?"
            ],
            [
              "Kayboldum.",
              "I'm lost."
            ],
            [
              "Burası neresi?",
              "Where is this place?"
            ],
            [
              "Haritada gösterebilir misiniz?",
              "Could you show me on the map?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "Answers you'll hear"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Türkçe",
            "English",
            "Türkçe",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "düz git / gidin",
              "go straight on",
              "sağa dön / dönün",
              "turn right"
            ],
            [
              "sola dön / dönün",
              "turn left",
              "geri dön / dönün",
              "turn back"
            ],
            [
              "karşıya geç / geçin",
              "cross over",
              "yukarı çık / çıkın",
              "go up"
            ],
            [
              "aşağı in / inin",
              "go down",
              "burada dur / durun",
              "stop here"
            ],
            [
              "ilk sokak",
              "first street",
              "ikinci sokak",
              "second street"
            ],
            [
              "köşe",
              "corner",
              "trafik ışığı",
              "traffic light"
            ],
            [
              "yaklaşık",
              "about",
              "metre",
              "meter"
            ],
            [
              "yakın / uzak",
              "near / far",
              "karşısında",
              "opposite it"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Notice that directions take the *-e* suffix: *sağ-a dön* (turn TO the right), *sol-a*, *yukarı-ya*, *karşı-ya*. This is the direction suffix from unit 8, working exactly as it does with place names."
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Ordinal numbers",
          "items": [
            "\"First, second, third\" are formed with *-inci / -ıncı / -uncu / -üncü*: *birinci*, *ikinci*, *üçüncü*, *dördüncü*, *beşinci*, *altıncı*, *yedinci*, *sekizinci*, *dokuzuncu*, *onuncu*.",
            "They're used for floors (*üçüncü kat*), streets (*ikinci sokak*) and dates (*ayın on beşinci günü*)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Unit 13 review",
          "items": [
            "Cover the Turkish and rebuild these from memory: \"What do you usually do in the mornings?\" · \"I usually get up early.\" · \"I don't smoke.\" · \"We drink tea every day.\" · \"Will you have another glass?\"",
            "Then make the aorist negative: *gelirim*, *yaparsın*, *içeriz*."
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
